<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sudeten German Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sudetendeutsche Partei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sudeten German Party was the political party of the German minority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-henlein">Konrad Henlein</a> in 1933 as the Sudeten German Home Front and renamed in 1935. Winning more votes than any other party in the 1935 elections, it was increasingly directed and funded by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its demands for autonomy led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a>, and it merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in late 1938.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sudeten-german-party.jpg" alt="Sudeten German Party" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FDRMRZUSA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Sudeten_German_Party.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party's forerunners went back to the German Workers' Party founded in 1903 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudeten-germans">Sudeten Germans</a> in the Bohemian crown lands of the Austro-Hungarian Monarchy, a pan-German and anti-Slav party centred on Eger and Aussig. Renamed the German National Socialist Workers' Party (DNSAP) in 1918, it demanded that the German-settled borderlands join German-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and with the German National Party it led the so-called negativism of Sudeten Germans who refused to accept the Czechoslovak state. Both parties dissolved themselves in 1933 to forestall a ban by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> government, and the Sudeten German Home Front was founded on 1 October 1933.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-henlein">Konrad Henlein</a> at first rejected any link with the DNSAP. He favoured the corporative ideas of Othmar Spann and the Austrian model, and until 1937 his speeches stressed loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>; he later called his contacts with Nazi leaders merely tactical. After Karl Hermann Frank became deputy leader in 1935, the party grew more radical. In the May 1935 election it won 1,249,534 votes, 15.2 per cent, and about 68 per cent of the German vote, while groups within it were increasingly financed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> declared his intention to separate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a>, as recorded in the Hossbach Memorandum of November 1937, the party coordinated its policy with Nazi leaders. Henlein first met Hitler on 28 March 1938, the Farmers' League joined the party that month, and at Carlsbad on 24 April the party demanded self-government for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudeten-germans">Sudeten Germans</a> and the freedom to choose annexation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. By June 1938 it had over 1.3 million members, and in the communal elections of May 1938 it took 88 per cent of German votes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a>, Henlein was made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichskommissar">Reichskommissar</a> of the annexed territory on 1 October 1938, and the party was merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> at a ceremony in Reichenberg (Liberec) on 5 November 1938. Officials such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinhard-heydrich">Reinhard Heydrich</a> distrusted its members, who had to apply individually; about 520,000 were admitted. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">mandates</a> of 46 deputies and 22 senators of the party and its Carpathian German ally were annulled on 30 October 1938, and its branches left in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> formed the German People's Group in Czecho-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sudeten German Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was the only democracy in Central Europe to last until 1938, but centralism angered Slovak autonomists such as Andrej Hlinka, and the <b>Sudeten German Party</b> of Konrad Henlein won more votes than any other party in 1935.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
